  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a matter-wave interferometry setup for nanoparticles using three gratings and a laser. On the left, many nanoparticles enter the apparatus. They first pass through a coherence grating, which appears to prepare or spatially organize the particle beam so the particles can behave coherently. In the middle, a laser forms a diffraction grating that splits and spreads the nanoparticle matter waves, shown by curved wavefronts and an interference-like pattern. This creates a structured diffraction pattern between the gratings.\n\nThe particles then reach a detection grating on the right, where the interference pattern is converted into a measurable signal. The small plot at the far right shows the key outcome: the number of detected particles oscillates periodically with position on the detection grating. This indicates alternating maxima and minima, consistent with an interference fringe pattern.\n\nOverall, the image conveys how nanoparticles can be manipulated like waves: prepared for coherence, diffracted by a laser-generated grating, and finally measured through position-dependent particle counts. The central message is the observation of wave interference in nanoparticles through a grating-based detection scheme.",
